      "claim": "Atmospheric pressure and density decrease monotonically with increasing altitude.",
      "evidence": "Hydrostatic equilibrium and the ideal‑gas law give the barometric formula P = P₀ exp(‑M g h / R T); density ρ = P/(R T) follows the same exponential decline.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Measured air density at the surface (~1.225 kg·m⁻³) is greater than densities at the base of the stratosphere (~0.4 kg·m⁻³) and all higher layers, so the troposphere contains the greatest density of any atmospheric layer.",
      "evidence": "Empirical atmospheric data tables show the rapid drop in density with height; ~75–80 % of the total atmospheric mass resides within the troposphere.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore, the best description of the troposphere among the options is that it has the greatest density.",
      "evidence": "Steps 1‑3 establish that the troposphere is the lowest layer and that density is highest near the surface, making option B the correct choice.",
